 =?UTF-8?q?om=20sidebar=20cards?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Behaviours:
- A thread card (flat list, pinned cards, cards under project headers and the Working shelf) shows one icon-only read-state action in its hover and focus-visible action cluster, directly before Snooze: Mark read (lucide mail-check) while the thread counts as unread for the sidebar, otherwise Mark unread (lucide mail-open, the icon the context menu already used). It has the Snooze button's size, hit area, pointer, tooltip placement and focus behaviour, an accessible name equal to its tooltip, and no label text or new colour. Slim rows and the folder variants do not show it.
- The action is decided by resolveSidebarThreadReadAction from the same resolveSidebarThreadTopStatus derivation that gives a row its Done or Woke state and the project badges their counts: mark-read when the thread has an unseen completion or an unacknowledged wake, mark-unread when it has a completed turn, and no action otherwise (no turn yet, or a first turn still running), because the server rejects mark-unread without a completed run. A thread with an unseen wake and no completed turn keeps Mark read, which is how Woke is dismissed from the card.
- Mark read sends what the open thread's visit tracker sends, without navigating and without counting as an open: a thread.visit command with visitedAt set to the later of the shell's updatedAt and its wake time. The server moves lastVisitedAt forward and broadcasts the visited thread shell, so every connected device sees the thread as read. A thread from a server without visit tracking (lastVisitedAt undefined) gets the local visit watermark instead, as the tracker does. Because the visit is at or after the wake, it also clears Woke, and the project badges and collapsed-header badges follow because they read the same state.
- Mark unread from the card is the existing mark-unread path (the server's markUnread command, or the local rewind before the server config loads).
- The action does not require the operate permission, mirroring the context menu, where mark-read and mark-unread are both exempt in threadActionRequiresOperate. A click prevents default and stops propagation, so it neither opens nor selects the row; a click without movement does not start a drag.
- The button stops propagation of pointerdown, so a press on it never reaches the row's sortable listeners: pressing it and moving past the drag threshold does not start a row drag or swallow the click. It does not start the sweep gesture that Settle starts on pointerdown.
- The single-thread menus (the sidebar row menu and the chat header menu) take the same three-state decision as the card from readThreadReadAction: Mark read, Mark unread, or no read item when the thread has no completed run and nothing unseen, so they never offer a Mark unread the server would reject. They dispatch to the same markThreadRead and markThreadUnread actions as the card. The DOM context menu fallback draws the mail-check icon. The multi-selection menu is unchanged and still offers only Mark unread.
- The user guide describes Mark read and Mark unread in two sentences next to the other thread actions, without describing when each label shows or promising cross-device reach.

Port to e07585e5fb:
- Sidebar.tsx: applyThreadReadAction sits before upstream's routeTarget, which now comes from useShownThreadRouteTarget so a Settings or Usage modal keeps the thread behind it current.
- useThreadActionMenu.ts: the mark-read case follows upstream's regenerate-title case, which now shows a title regeneration toast in its own block.

